$ yo explain E0904
E0904 — not supported in an io.async body that awaits

An `io.async` body that awaits is lowered to a state machine, and a few constructs cannot be placed in one yet.

An `io.await` must stand as a statement, as the value of `x := ...` or `x = ...`, as a `cond`/`match` arm value, as the FIRST `cond` condition, or inside a `while` body; hoist any other await into a local first (`r := io.await(f, io);`). An `if` whose body awaits must be written as the `cond` it expands to. A `match` whose arms await takes only plain variant patterns: bind the payload and match again inside the arm. An `inout(name) := ...` binding cannot live in such a body, because the state machine keeps a per-state copy of each local; read the value into a local instead.

Example — this fails:
  inner :: (fn(io : Io, n : i32) -> Impl(Future(i32)))(io.async((e : Io) => (n + i32(1))));
  outer :: (fn(io : Io) -> Impl(Future(i32)))(
    io.async((e : Io) => {
      x := i32(1);
      inout(y) := x;
      z := e.await(inner(e, y), e);
      y = z;
      x
    })
  );
  main :: (fn(io : Io) -> unit)({
    _v := io.await(outer(io), io);
  });
  export(main);
  
Example — write this instead:
  inner :: (fn(io : Io, n : i32) -> Impl(Future(i32)))(io.async((e : Io) => (n + i32(1))));
  outer :: (fn(io : Io) -> Impl(Future(i32)))(
    io.async((e : Io) => {
      x := i32(1);
      z := e.await(inner(e, x), e);
      (x + z)
    })
  );
  main :: (fn(io : Io) -> unit)({
    _v := io.await(outer(io), io);
  });
  export(main);
  
  The await-position rules are enforced where the state machine is built, so `yo compile` reports them; the `inout` binding is reported by `yo check` too.
